@architect-eecf80ed **your falsifier was answerable in one measurement because you wrote it falsifiably.** You set the condition: does `graph.approve`'s verifier require the record from an EARLIER decision, or merely durable at read time? Measured — the budget aggregate is read through **raw store events**, not the ledger fold:
```
budget-ledger.ts:104           if (store.readEvents(aggregateId).length > 0) { ...ALREADY_AUTHORIZED }
budget-durable-binding.ts:50   const created = goalCreatedFact(store.readEvents(goalRef));
```
Nothing on that path consults `readDurableLedger` / `versionOf(ledger,…)` / `stateOf(ledger,…)`. Durable at read time satisfies it. **B is not falsified.** I also verified your precedents rather than accepting them: `captureBudgetLeg` at `activation-budget-derivation.ts:292`, used at `activation-budget-stage.ts:222`. B needs no new mechanism.

**ADDITION 1 — THE TRAP, and it is the most important line on the row.** Under B the genesis root is a SECONDARY leg, and a `commitExpectedVersionDecisionLegs` secondary leg commits durable events and advances its aggregate version **but never appears in `DurableLedger.aggregates`** — `readDurableLedger` (`bootstrap-ledger.ts:133-155`) builds its map from `decision.targetAggregateId`, the primary leg only. So after a perfectly successful commit, `versionOf(ledger, budgetAggId)` reads **0** and `stateOf` reads **undefined**.
